Tommi Syrjanen wrote:
> Ken Cuvelier <kvcflameNO@SPAMyahoo.com> writes:
> 
> 
>>I don't know about you, but for me it's no challenge whatsoever to
>>avoid using rusty iron keys to hit black or brown puddings until an
>>entire dungeon level is filled with them, then mindlessly harvesting
>>them for hours and hours over a walled-in altar.
> 
> 
> And I don't know about you, but for me it's no challenge whatsoever to
> avoid genociding monsters.  Or avoid polymorphing objects. Or avoid
> polymorping myself. But people still count "genocideless",
> "polypileless", and "polyselfless" as conducts.

You are being disengenuous here, not to mention that you are listing 
conducts that the game itself reports.  It is harder to ascend a 
character while adhering to the conducts you have listed for various 
reasons, and players will respect you for being able to ascend while 
doing so.  While seeing a 10^8-range score followed by a dumplog that 
clearly indicates pudding farming was used turns me off, I don't 
consider that the player broke a conduct to get there.

> 
> Sure, avoiding farming is a trivial conduct (like avoiding
> polymorphing objects), but it still is a conduct.

Let's agree to disagree then.  But I'd like to point out that your list 
requires careful active play - there is nothing careful or particularly 
active about playing farmless; rather the opposite, in fact, because to 
use the pudding farm loophole successfully you have to do many things 
very carefully.

In closing, consider a straightforward game in which you assemble an 
ascension kit, go get the invocation artifacts, fetch the Amulet and run 
back to the planes.  This is a huge accomplishment in and of itself.  If 
you do all that without adhering to the standard, in-game-recognized 
conducts, it's still an accomplishment.  Does pudding farming make it 
easier to do it?  Debatable, because in and of itself, pudding farming 
is difficult to do.  It is difficult to argue, though, that adhering to 
the official conducts does not make things harder.  This is the main 
reason I contend that "farmless" is not a voluntary challenge.

-Ken


